            select c;
    }

   return someQueryable.ToList();
</code></pre>

Q: Entity framework, repository/service with ability to read/write at same time A: <blockquote>
<p>You dont need seperate <code>context</code> for Read and write.</p>
</blockquote>
<pre><code>public IQueryable&lt;Product&gt; GetAll()
{
    return context.products;
}
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>The <code>IQueryable</code> interface inherits from the <code>IEnumerable</code> interface so that if it represents a query, the results of that query can be enumerated.<br />
Enumeration causes the expression tree associated with an <code>IQueryable</code> object to be executed</p>
</blockquote>
<p>So that you need to execute it.</p>
<pre><code>// Approach 1
public void Run()
{
    var products = Repository.GetAll().ToList();
    foreach (var product in products)
        Repository.AddEvent();
}

// Having condition in IQueryable
public void Run()
{
    var products = Repository
                  .GetAll();
                  .Where(someCondition)
                  .OrderBy(SomeOrder)
                  .ToList();   // At this point, EF will materialize the query 
                               // and process in SQL and you will get the 
                               // result applying this condition in SQL
    foreach (var product in products)
        Repository.AddEvent();
}
</code></pre>
<p>Pros</p>
<ul>
<li>It will NOT have multiple roundtrip to database</li>
<li>It will fetch data in single shot</li>
</ul>
<p>Cons</p>
<ul>
<li>It will fetch data to memory</li>
</ul>
<p>Anyway if you iterate you will load that in to the memory, so it's better to avoid expensive I/O operation.</p>
<p>You have to close the reader associated with the connection before starting a new transaction.<a href="https://blogs.msdn.microsoft.com/bill/2006/06/12/system-data-sqlclient-sqlexception-3988/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">msdn</a></p>
<p>Keep in mind the Entity Framework <code>DbContext</code> is not thread-safe</p>

Q: Creating a generic method in IEnumerable A: <p>I think I can solve this by just changing the methods around a bit like this:</p>
<pre><code>public static class LinqExtensions
{
    public static IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; Yield&lt;T&gt;(this IEnumerable&lt;T&gt; models, int numberResults) =&gt; models.AsQueryable().YieldQueryable(numberResults);
    public static IQueryable&lt;T&gt; Yield&lt;T&gt;(this IQueryable&lt;T&gt; models, int numberResults) =&gt; models.YieldQueryable(numberResults);

    /// &lt;summary&gt;
    /// Lists database entities by a number of results or lists them all 
    /// &lt;/summary&gt;
    /// &lt;typeparam name=&quot;T&quot;&gt;The generic entity to list&lt;/typeparam&gt;
    /// &lt;param name=&quot;models&quot;&gt;The query to yield&lt;/param&gt;
    /// &lt;param name=&quot;numberResults&quot;&gt;The number of results to yield&lt;/param&gt;
    /// &lt;returns&gt;&lt;/returns&gt;
    private static IQueryable&lt;T&gt; YieldQueryable&lt;T&gt;(this IQueryable&lt;T&gt; models, int numberResults) =&gt; numberResults &gt; 0 ? models.Take(numberResults) : models;
}
</code></pre>
<p>It compiles and the users are still returned as <code>IQueryable</code> so it looks like it works.</p>
